== 3. The three cargo legs, and the GUI check

Those four legs are the expensive ones and the ones no host-side command reaches:

    == cargo check    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 3m 34s
    == cargo clippy   Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 2m 52s
    == cargo test     386 `test result:` blocks, listed in section 4
    == GUI protocol types
    GUI protocol types are up to date

`cargo clippy` is run with `-D warnings`, and the section between its heading and the next one
carries zero lines starting with `warning`. That matters for the reading of section 4: the test
counts below are taken from a tree that also compiles clean under the lint set, so a passing
test is not standing in for a build problem elsewhere.

== 4. The size of the test leg

    $ grep -c "^test result: ok" /var/tmp/verify-full-a04e91a7.log
    386
    $ grep -oE "^test result: ok\. [0-9]+ passed; [0-9]+ failed; [0-9]+ ignored" \
          /var/tmp/verify-full-a04e91a7.log | awk -F'[ ;]+' \
          '{p+=$4; f+=$6; i+=$8} END {print "passed:", p, " failed:", f, " ignored:", i}'
    passed: 31593  failed: 0  ignored: 485
    $ grep -c "^test result: FAILED" /var/tmp/verify-full-a04e91a7.log
    0

The leg is invoked with `--no-fail-fast` (`cargo test --workspace --locked --no-fail-fast`), so
"0 failed" is a conclusion drawn after all 386 blocks were run rather than a sweep that stopped
at the first red test.

The 485 ignored break down as 436 unit and integration tests plus 49 doctests, which cargo
reports in the same line shape (`test path/to/lib.rs - item (line 15) ... ignored`):

    $ grep "\.\.\. ignored" /var/tmp/verify-full-a04e91a7.log | grep -v "\.rs - " | wc -l
    436
    $ grep -E "^test [^ ]+\.rs - .* \.\.\. ignored" /var/tmp/verify-full-a04e91a7.log | wc -l
    49

That is not the same population as the `ignored-test baseline` gate's "429 total", which counts
`#[ignore]` attributes in the tracked sources and so sees neither the doctests nor an attribute
that the scan cannot attach to a test function. The two numbers answer different questions and
are not expected to be equal.

== 5. Two fixtures skipped inside the container, and why

The full run is green, but two of its own fixtures declined to check anything, and both are
worth stating rather than leaving buried in an `OK (skipped=1)`:

    test_verdicts_agree_with_a_real_yaml_parser (__main__.WorkflowYaml...) ...
        skipped 'PyYAML is not installed on this host'
    note: PyYAML absent -- running the check's own assertions only
    Ran 7 tests in 0.365s
    OK (skipped=1)

    test_the_dependency_unwraps_the_lookup (__main__.Premise...) ...
        skipped 'the prometheus source is not vendored on this host'
    Ran 33 tests in 16.991s
    OK (skipped=1)

Neither is a hole in this run that a host run closes; both skip in the container and in CI for
the same reason, and in each case the gate keeps working without it. `check-workflow-yaml.py`
parses `.github/workflows/*.yml` with its own line-oriented reader, and the skipped fixture is
the one that cross-checks that reader against a real YAML parser; `check-metric-labels.py` cites
`prometheus-0.14.0` source for its premise, and the skipped fixture is the one that reads the
`unwrap` out of the vendored crate. Both fixtures do run where the dependency is present, which
is why the premises are still checked somewhere:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-workflow-yaml.py 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 7 tests in 0.383s

    OK
    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-metric-labels.py \
          Premise.test_the_dependency_unwraps_the_lookup 2>&1 | tail -3
    Ran 1 test in 0.062s

    OK

Seven tests with no skip line means PyYAML was importable and the cross-check ran; the same
seven in the container carry `skipped 'PyYAML is not installed on this host'`. An image carrying
PyYAML and the prometheus sources would make the container leg strictly stronger at the cost of
no longer being a `debian:bookworm-slim` with a Rust toolchain and nothing else, and that
trade-off belongs to whoever maintains the image rather than to this run.

== 6. Reproducing it, and what this record is not

    $ git clone <this repository> /var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7
    $ cd /var/tmp/verify-frozen-a04e91a7 && git checkout --detach a04e91a7
    $ IMAGE_TAG=chaos-verify:frozen ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full

What the run does not cover:

- This is `a04e91a7`, the commit before the `timeout children` gate. The `gates` array at that
  commit has 33 entries and does not include `check-timeout-child.py`; that gate, its two fixed
  call sites and its behavioural test are recorded separately in
  `docs/verification/timeout-child-2026-10-04.log`. This run therefore says nothing about them.
- Not a cold cache, and not a baseline. See section 1 about the named volumes.
- Linux only. The entry point is a Debian bookworm-slim container; the macOS and Windows
  conclusions come from their own runners, recorded in
  `docs/verification/platform-ci-2026-10-02.log` and
  `docs/verification/windows-test-failures-2026-10-04.log`.
- No browser. Nothing in the 33 gates drives a UI, and the image has no node_modules and no
  browser binary, so "all gates passed" and "the TUI and the desktop flows behave" remain two
  separate conclusions.
